  data:  # 5806
    kind: work_item  # 5806
    title: "No persistent way to enable a fidelity backend: the only durable mechanism (a global env var) has the wrong scope for a per-repository trust decision, and the opt-in nudge cannot be dismissed"  # 5806
    status: todo_hard  # 5806
    priority: 2  # 5806
    parent:  # 5806
    tags:  # 5806
    - multi_fidelity  # 5806
    - rust  # 5806
    - usability  # 5806
    - security  # 5806
    isbefore: []  # 5806
    duplicate_of: []  # 5806
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 5806
    pr_ref:  # 5806
    description: "RAISED BY THE OWNER 2026-08-23, in their words: \"is there a way for the user to\npermanently enable rust-analyzer? if not, there should be\".\n\nANSWER, read in code: THERE IS NO PERSISTENT ENABLE. The gate\n(hypergumbo_lang_rust_analyzer/gate.py) accepts exactly two inputs -- the\nHYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER env var, and --backend rust-analyzer, which cli.py\nnormalises INTO that same env var at 11311/11319. hypergumbo has NO user\nconfiguration file of any kind: there is no ~/.config/hypergumbo, no\nhypergumbo.toml, no per-repo config reader. (`.hypergumbo` in discovery.py is an\nOUTPUT-artifact name in the ignore list, not config.) `install-rust-analyzer`\ninstalls the binary via rustup and stops; it enables nothing.\n\nSo the only durable mechanism available today is exporting\nHYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1 from a shell profile.\n\nWHY THAT WORKAROUND IS THE WRONG SHAPE, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THIS ITEM. The\nthing being gated is not a preference, it is a TRUST decision: enabling the\nbackend causes rust-analyzer to execute the analysed workspace's build.rs and\nproc macros AS THE INVOKING USER. The tool says so in its own warning text, and\nAGENTS.md carries the standing rule \"rust-analyzer EXECUTES build.rs -- never on\nuntrusted repos\".\n\nA trust decision is PER-REPOSITORY. The env var is GLOBAL. Exporting it from a\nprofile silently opts every Rust repository the user ever analyses -- including\nones cloned to look at, audit, or triage -- into arbitrary code execution. So\nthe only persistence hypergumbo offers today has exactly the wrong scope for the\nsecurity property it gates, and the tool never mentions the workaround, probably\nfor that reason. That is a missing FEATURE, not a missing docs line.\n\nSECOND, SMALLER PROBLEM: THE NUDGE IS UNDISMISSABLE. check_rust_analyzer_\ndisclosure fires the \"installed but NOT enabled\" arm on EVERY run against EVERY\nRust repo whenever the binary is on PATH, and a user who has deliberately\ndecided to stay on tree-sitter has no way to say so. The project has already\nwritten down why that matters, in TestRustAnalyzerDisclosureRespectsTheGate:\n\"A nudge that fires when it is already moot trains people to skim past the one\nsentence that must land.\" That reasoning was applied to the backend-active case\nand fixed there; it applies equally to a user who has answered the question and\nkeeps being asked.\n\nDESIGN CONSTRAINT FOR WHOEVER SCOPES THIS -- the trust record MUST NOT live\ninside the analysed repository. A repo that ships its own \"trust me\" marker is\nprecisely the attack this gate exists to prevent. The established shape is\ndirenv's: the opt-in is recorded in the USER's own state directory, keyed by\nabsolute path (and ideally a content hash, so the grant does not survive the\nmanifest changing under it). VS Code workspace trust and mise/asdf trust\nprompts are the same pattern.\n\nSUGGESTED SHAPE, not a decision:\n  * `hypergumbo trust-backend rust-analyzer <path>` (and a `--forget`), writing\n    to a user-level store outside every repo;\n  * gate.py consults it as a THIRD input after env and flag;\n  * the existing nudge gains a \"make this permanent for this repo: <command>\"\n    line and goes quiet for paths already decided either way;\n  * a global always-on remains possible but should require naming itself as\n    such, not be reachable by exporting an env var whose scope the user has not\n    thought about.\n\nBROADER THAN RUST, and this is why it is filed as its own item rather than on\nWI-gojum: hypergumbo has no user-config mechanism at all, so this is the first\none, and every later fidelity backend (WI-nanom's pyright, tsserver, gopls,\nJDT) will want the same switch. pyright executes nothing, so its opt-in is a\npreference and not a trust grant -- the store should not assume every backend\ncarries build.rs's hazard.\n\nNOT MEASURED: whether any user has actually hit this (no telemetry, and the\nfinding came from an authored fixture, not a report)."  # 5806
    fields: {}  # 5806
- op: discuss  # 76f3
  at: '2026-08-24T01:51:31Z'  # 76f3
  by: agent  # 76f3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 76f3
  clock: 2  # 76f3
  nonce: 76f3  # 76f3
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-08-23 resolving ADR-0045 OQ1, in their words: chose \"Split:\nrevoke on build.rs only\" over leaving it advisory and over full strictness.\n\nA changed build.rs REVOKES a trust grant; a changed Cargo.toml does NOT.\nImplemented and verified end-to-end on an authored fixture: granted repo -> 12\norigin=scip nodes; Cargo.toml bumped -> still granted, still 12, change\nsurfaced on --show; build.rs edited -> 0 scip nodes and the modified script\nnever ran.\n\nTHE LIMIT WAS ON THE TABLE WHEN THE RULING WAS MADE and is recorded on the ADR\nrather than left implicit: only TOP-LEVEL manifests are hashed, so no form of\nthis protects against a DEPENDENCY's build script. It catches tampering with\nthis project's own build script and nothing else. Strictness therefore buys\nless than it appears to, which is part of why full strictness (re-prompting on\nevery dependency bump) was not worth its habituation cost.\n\nADR-0045 amended in place: ruling 7 rewritten, OQ1 marked RESOLVED,\nalternative A2 re-scoped to say why full strictness lost."  # 76f3
- op: discuss  # 546e
  at: '2026-09-18T08:41:59Z'  # 546e
  by: agent  # 546e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 546e
  clock: 3  # 546e
  nonce: 546e  # 546e
  message: "ORDERING CONSTRAINT DISCOVERED 2026-09-18, and this row turns out to explain a\nfive-month blind spot.\n\nWHY THE BACKEND'S DEFECTS WENT UNSEEN. This row establishes that there is NO\npersistent way to enable the rust-analyzer backend -- the gate reads one env var,\nthere is no config file of any kind. The consequence, which this row does not yet\nrecord: **in ordinary use this arm never runs.** No CI job sets the variable, no\nbakeoff sets it, and a user must export it from a shell profile. So nothing that\nroutinely looks at hypergumbo output has ever looked at this backend's output.\n\nWhat was sitting behind that flag, measured this week at d6174d5df9:\n  - 329 of 455 local-pointing edges are provably false cross-file references --\n    21.2% of every edge in the artifact (filed 2026-09-18)\n  - 498 of 1255 Symbols share a stable_id, 39.7% (WI-jikok)\n  - cross-backend stable_id parity: 0 of 52 (filed 2026-09-18)\n  - the pass was until recently in NEITHER analysis_runs NOR skipped_passes,\n    orphaning 669 nodes and 637 edges (WI-didag, fixed PR #1030)\n\nThe WI-didag one is the tell: it was found only because someone deliberately\nturned the flag on, a month after it shipped. This is the LIVE.md rule \"MEASURE\nACROSS CONFIGURATIONS, NOT JUST REPOS\" as a lived consequence -- an audit at\ndefault settings cannot see a branch an opt-in flag opens.\n\nTHE CONSTRAINT, and it is the reason for this note. This row's fix makes the\nbackend durably reachable -- a per-repository trust decision a user can persist.\nThat is the right shape. But it promotes the surface above from \"unreachable by\naccident\" to \"on by deliberate user choice\", and a user who opts in today gets an\nartifact where a fifth of the edges are false.\n\n**The false-edge row should land before this one.** Filed as `isbefore` rather\nthan left as prose, so the dependency is machine-visible.\n\nNot a reason to deprioritise this row -- the absence of a persistent enable is\nALSO why the arm is under-measured, so fixing it is part of how the remaining\ndefects get found. It is a sequencing constraint, not an objection."  # 546e
